    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Brice Turang** (72.02) and **Corbin Carroll** (72.42) anchor our offensive production, serving as our highest-rated starters. **Brice Turang** is particularly well-positioned against the southpaw Kyle Freeland; the combination of a favorable park factor (+14%) and the pitcher\u2019s slow delivery significantly boosts his stolen base upside, a critical lever in our scoring format. Meanwhile, **Corbin Carroll** benefits from a strong platoon advantage against Cade Cavalli, bolstered by an optimal #2 order slot (+8%) that maximizes his exposure to run-scoring opportunities. We are also leaning heavily on **Tyler Soderstrom** (66.20), whose 4-hole projection against Burrows remains one of our most stable sources of power and RBI efficiency.\n\nOur most difficult bench decision involved **Austin Riley** (43.77), who narrowly missed the cut behind the utility slot occupied by **Vinnie Pasquantino**. While **Austin Riley** carries a high base projection, his matchup against the tough Mason Montgomery (3.20 xERA) resulted in a massive -19% penalty to his SP Skill metric, effectively cratering his efficiency relative to the rest of the infield. Elsewhere, **Jose Caballero** (60.15) remains a high-variance, high-reward play despite batting 8th; his elite sprint speed and a +13% platoon edge against Suarez, combined with favorable wind conditions, make him a superior selection to deeper-bench options like **Seiya Suzuki** or **Travis Bazzana**.\n\nRisk management remains a primary concern as we navigate several \"Lineup Pending\" designations. **Dalton Rushing**, **Gabriel Moreno**, **Alex Freeland**, **Kyle Karros**, and **James Wood** all carry the risk of mid-day lineup adjustments that could shift their projected scores. Specifically, **Dalton Rushing** and **Alex Freeland** are fighting to stay above our 40-point floor; should they drop lower in the batting order than our #8 and #9 assumptions, we may need to monitor their plate appearances closely to protect our daily game caps. **Mickey Moniak** continues to be held in an IL slot, leaving our bench depth reliant on the volatility of our lower-order bats.",
